window.docContents[35].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow--Mass-and-incompressibility":{"contents":"Conservation of mass is the continuity equation\n\n\n\nThe mass flux is , and the continuity residual isThe material derivative of density is\n.\n\nFor differentiable density and velocity components, the product rule gives\n\nApply the product rule to each component\n, sum over the spatial coordinates, and collect the\nterms . The remaining sum is\n.\n\nIf  is constant, thenEvery divergence-free flow therefore conserves constant density. When\n, the continuity equation and incompressibility are equivalent.\n\nThe time derivative of a constant vanishes, and constant scalar\nmultiplication passes through every spatial partial derivative. Thus the\ncontinuity residual is  times the divergence. If  is\nnonzero, their product vanishes exactly when the divergence vanishes.\n\nIncompressibility, , says that an infinitesimal fluid volume\nneither expands nor contracts.\n\nWrite spacetime as , with coordinate zero reserved for\ntime. The material derivative isand incompressibility sums only the spatial derivatives. The global condition\nrequires this sum to vanish everywhere; its pointwise form imposes the same\nidentity at one spacetime point.An incompressible solution pairs an equation-specific momentum predicate with\nincompressibility and initial data. A residual solution requires only those\nequations; a classical solution adds the regularity that makes the totalized\nderivatives analytic derivatives and the assigned initial value a one-sided\ntrace. Global solutions restrict canonically to any positive finite horizon.\nEuler and Navier–Stokes are both instances of these definitions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Conservation and Incompressibility\u0009From Conservation Laws to Incompressible Flow","header":"8.2.1.2. Mass and incompressibility","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow--Mass-and-incompressibility"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--Spaces___-exponents___-and-boundary-data":{"contents":"The parameters that matter stay in the theorem statements: the open domain\n, the derivative order , the exponent , the ambient dimension,\nand the measure. The completed graph-space model keeps derivative coordinates as\n classes, which is what separates an almost-everywhere class from any\nparticular continuous or pointwise representative.\n\n membership of every output does not by itself\ngive a bounded operator, and almost-everywhere agreement identifies a selected\nrepresentative with an  class without turning the class into a\npointwise function. Trace values and singular quotients such as\n are defined on the completed space\nby density and continuity, never by evaluating an arbitrary representative.\n\nBoundary theorems use a finite collection of\nboundary charts with controlled derivatives.\nEach chart flattens a piece of the boundary into a coordinate hyperplane;\ncutoff functions localize the function to those pieces and assemble the local\nextensions. Bounds on the charts and their inverses let us transfer Sobolev\nestimates between the original domain and the flattened coordinates. We ask\nfor these maps and bounds explicitly because the extension, trace, and\ncompactness proofs use them quantitatively. A separate geometric theorem\nconstructs this data for a bounded open set with  graph boundary. The\ninterval and the Euclidean unit ball are the concrete instances.\n\nZero boundary values come from closures of compactly supported smooth\nfunctions:  and the higher-order  model. The embedding\ntheorems carry their own exponent inequalities, which is what decides whether\nyou are in the subcritical, critical, or Morrey regime.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009The analytic language of weak solutions","header":"4.1.3. Spaces, exponents, and boundary data","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--Spaces___-exponents___-and-boundary-data"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation":{"contents":"In the opening heat calculation, the useful conclusion is an equality between\nderivatives of an ordinary real function. Computing a syntax tree for the\nanswer is an intermediate step. A soundness theorem connects that tree to the\nfunction and gives us an equality we can use in a proof.\n\n\n\nAn expression, interval, or polynomial becomes usable in a proof only after\nit is connected to the original function by a soundness theorem. The domain\nconditions travel with that theorem: a cancellation involving a denominator,\nfor instance, still needs the denominator to be nonzero at the points in\nquestion.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.4. Proof-producing computation","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation"}});